Check: $FD'=\sqrt{49+100}=\sqrt{149}=FD$, and $\overrightarrow{FD}\cdot\overrightarrow{FD'}=-70+70=0$, so the angle $DFD'$ is a right angle; going from $FD$ to $FD'$ is counterclockwise, as the arrow shows.

\textbf{Reflection.} The reflection of $(p,q)$ across $y=x-2$ is $(q+2,\,p-2)$: the midpoint $\left(\dfrac{p+q+2}{2},\dfrac{p+q-2}{2}\right)$ lies on the line, and the segment joining the point to its image has direction $(q+2-p,\,p-2-q)$, which is perpendicular to the line's direction $(1,1)$. So $R'=(3+2,\,-5-2)=(5,-7)$.

\textbf{Rotation.} A $90^\circ$ clockwise rotation about the origin sends $(p,q)$ to $(q,-p)$, so $R''=(-7,-5)$.

\begin{asy}
